WEBVTT

NOTE
English captions generated from the talk script, not transcribed from the
recording. Cues follow the slide changes and the pauses in the narration;
inside a slide their times are estimated from character counts.

1
00:00:00.437 --> 00:00:04.742
Hi, I'm Debjyoti Saha Roy
from Northeastern University.

2
00:00:04.742 --> 00:00:08.537
This is Don't Pay Attention,
PLANT It: pretraining attention

3
00:00:08.537 --> 00:00:13.510
via learning-to-rank, joint work
with Byron Wallace and Javed Aslam.

4
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:15.626
Here is the task.

5
00:00:15.626 --> 00:00:21.206
The input is text: a hospital discharge
summary, often well over a thousand words.

6
00:00:21.206 --> 00:00:24.839
The output is a set of labels:
the diagnosis codes that apply,

7
00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:27.441
like pneumonia, diabetes,
and heart failure here.

8
00:00:27.862 --> 00:00:30.337
That is extreme
multi-label classification.

9
00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:32.093
The code book is huge:

10
00:00:32.313 --> 00:00:35.429
ICD-10 defines about a hundred
and fifty-four thousand codes.

11
00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:38.518
And most codes are rare:
in a standard dataset,

12
00:00:38.518 --> 00:00:41.651
more than half appear
fewer than ten times.

13
00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:46.667
So why not just ask
a large language model?

14
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.039
You can. But the prompt
has to carry the label list,

15
00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:53.166
and the model's attention
gets spread thin.

16
00:00:53.166 --> 00:00:55.924
On the right, a 2025 study.

17
00:00:56.227 --> 00:00:58.883
A seventy-billion-parameter
reasoning model,

18
00:00:59.242 --> 00:01:01.864
with about a thousand codes in its prompt,

19
00:01:01.864 --> 00:01:07.054
trails a fine-tuned model two hundred
times smaller, on both scores.

20
00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:10.267
And the real code book is far longer.

21
00:01:10.412 --> 00:01:14.971
The standard recipe for thousands
of labels is to fine-tune:

22
00:01:14.971 --> 00:01:18.095
the labels move out of the prompt
and into the model.

23
00:01:18.095 --> 00:01:23.558
On top of the language model, every label
gets its own attention over the tokens,

24
00:01:23.558 --> 00:01:25.464
one spotlight per label.

25
00:01:25.464 --> 00:01:30.736
Here is the catch. Each spotlight starts
from random weights, the gray beams,

26
00:01:30.736 --> 00:01:33.763
and has to find the tokens
that matter, the green ones,

27
00:01:34.024 --> 00:01:36.841
taught only by the classification loss.

28
00:01:36.841 --> 00:01:40.076
A rare label has just
a handful of examples.

29
00:01:41.308 --> 00:01:46.480
Our idea is to plant that attention
before the classifier ever trains.

30
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.637
Same architecture, nothing added.

31
00:01:48.637 --> 00:01:51.808
In stage one, we cut
the pipeline before the classifier

32
00:01:51.808 --> 00:01:54.750
and train the label attention
as a learning-to-rank model:

33
00:01:55.044 --> 00:01:58.461
for each label, rank the tokens
by how informative they are.

34
00:01:58.461 --> 00:02:04.421
In stage two, we put the classifier back
and fine-tune everything end to end,

35
00:02:04.421 --> 00:02:06.833
as usual, starting
from the planted weights.

36
00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:09.856
Here is stage one, left to right.

37
00:02:09.856 --> 00:02:14.184
On the left, each label
queries the LLM's token states,

38
00:02:14.184 --> 00:02:17.269
and the label attention
gives each token a score.

39
00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:23.039
The orange box holds the tokens
that tell us most about this label:

40
00:02:23.039 --> 00:02:25.142
that is mutual information.

41
00:02:25.142 --> 00:02:30.587
Take a pair where j tells us more than h:
j should score higher.

42
00:02:30.958 --> 00:02:34.285
Magenta is the probability
that j ranks above h.

43
00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:40.274
Blue is the swap cost:
how much the ranking score, nDCG,

44
00:02:40.274 --> 00:02:42.178
would change if they swapped,

45
00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:45.313
so mistakes near the top cost more.

46
00:02:45.313 --> 00:02:48.531
The loss below sums this over all pairs.

47
00:02:48.531 --> 00:02:53.415
So attention learns to put
each label's most telling tokens first.

48
00:02:54.973 --> 00:03:00.402
Why does this help?
Count the signal reaching attention.

49
00:03:00.968 --> 00:03:04.943
Classification gives each label
one signal: right or wrong.

50
00:03:04.943 --> 00:03:09.588
Ranking compares pairs
among its top k tokens,

51
00:03:09.588 --> 00:03:11.958
so the signal grows like k squared.

52
00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:15.222
It tells attention which
tokens should outrank which.

53
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.880
So, is it worth it?

54
00:03:19.354 --> 00:03:23.464
This is diagnosis coding on MIMIC-IV,
a public hospital dataset.

55
00:03:23.464 --> 00:03:28.142
The vertical axis is Macro-F1,
where every code counts equally.

56
00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:34.120
Along the bottom are four language models,
from Phi-3-mini to DeepSeek-V3.

57
00:03:34.451 --> 00:03:37.995
Gray is the model fine-tuned alone;
green adds PLANT.

58
00:03:37.995 --> 00:03:41.946
Every one gains about three points.

59
00:03:42.782 --> 00:03:48.659
And the rare labels?
Here we train only on common labels,

60
00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:51.777
then test on rare labels
the model has never seen.

61
00:03:52.078 --> 00:03:55.249
The axis is F1 on those rare labels.

62
00:03:55.977 --> 00:03:59.980
With random attention, the gray bars,
every model is near zero.

63
00:04:00.511 --> 00:04:05.055
With planted attention, in green,
they reach seven to eight percent.

64
00:04:05.843 --> 00:04:10.519
The planted attention carries over to
labels with no training examples at all.

65
00:04:11.365 --> 00:04:16.544
To wrap up. Large language models
can do extreme classification.

66
00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:19.562
What holds them back is attention
that starts from scratch.

67
00:04:20.013 --> 00:04:21.365
PLANT is a plug-and-play,

68
00:04:21.365 --> 00:04:24.413
architecture-agnostic method
to plant that attention.

69
00:04:24.413 --> 00:04:26.118
One: plug-and-play.

70
00:04:26.492 --> 00:04:30.186
Pretrain the label attention as a ranker,
then fine-tune as usual.

71
00:04:30.186 --> 00:04:32.454
Two: architecture-agnostic.

72
00:04:32.454 --> 00:04:35.865
Modern LLM backbones, like Mistral,
Llama, DeepSeek and Phi-3,

73
00:04:35.865 --> 00:04:40.702
do better with PLANT than alone,
and rare labels gain the most.

74
00:04:40.702 --> 00:04:42.119
Three: across tasks.

75
00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:46.298
It also does well on legal topic
classification and Wikipedia tagging.

76
00:04:46.583 --> 00:04:50.468
The limits: PLANT needs labeled data,

77
00:04:50.468 --> 00:04:53.748
and adds twelve to twenty-eight
percent training time.

78
00:04:55.179 --> 00:04:59.377
So: don't just pay attention, plant it.

79
00:04:59.377 --> 00:05:02.179
Come find me at the poster
in Sydney: Poster Session 1,

80
00:05:02.179 --> 00:05:05.871
Tuesday the eighth of December, ten a.m.
to one p.m., Hall 1 to 4.

81
00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:08.748
Scan for the paper or the code. Thank you.
